# Staging E2E Test Environment Variables
#
# Copy to `.env.test` (gitignored) and fill in the real values:
#   cp tests/e2e/staging/.env.test.example tests/e2e/staging/.env.test
#
# WHERE THE LIVE VALUES COME FROM
# -------------------------------
# The authoritative copies are the `TEST_USER_EMAIL` / `TEST_USER_PASSWORD`
# secrets in the repo's `staging` GitHub environment, which is what CI uses.
# GitHub secrets cannot be read back, so you cannot copy them out — ask the
# maintainer, or rotate the account password and set both this file and the
# secret to the new value (`gh secret set TEST_USER_PASSWORD --env staging`).
#
# ALWAYS SINGLE-QUOTE THE VALUES
# ------------------------------
# Both are read by `dotenv` in playwright.config.ts, and an UNQUOTED value is
# silently mangled by two different rules (#558, #599):
#
#   * dotenv treats an unquoted `#` as the start of an inline comment, so
#     `PASSWORD=abc#def` parses as `abc` — a truncated password that fails to
#     authenticate with no hint as to why. This has really happened here: a
#     32-character password parsed down to 4.
#   * anything that `source`s or `. `s this file gets bash expansion, so an
#     unquoted `$` interpolates as a variable — corrupting the value and
#     printing a fragment of it in the resulting error.
#
# Single quotes stop both. Prefer generating passwords without a literal `'`,
# since a single quote cannot be escaped inside single quotes in a .env file.
#
# The placeholder below is deliberately under eight characters. The repo's
# pre-commit secret scan flags a password-ish key assigned a quoted literal of
# eight or more characters, so a realistic-looking placeholder trips it — and so
# does spelling that rule out longhand, which is why this note does not. Put the
# real value in `.env.test`, which is gitignored and never scanned.

STAGING_TEST_EMAIL='you@example.com'
STAGING_TEST_PASSWORD='REPLACE'

# Optional: override the base URL to test a different environment.
# BASE_URL='https://dev.autoauthor.app'
